From 6cfbb2f2b43a46aefc312a12358ed2d70dff3ef3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 15:52:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] ipa: softisp: awb: Average the zones that could be grey

Grey world over the whole frame lets the dominant colour of a scene set the
white balance: on the Surface Pro 12 rear camera, a warm wooden floor and
cardboard turned white paper cyan.

Collect R, G and B sums over a 16x12 grid of zones in the software ISP
statistics, next to the global sums. The zones count every pixel; the
global sums keep excluding saturated ones. With a `zoneDistance` in the
tuning file, the AWB statistics take their colour from the zones that
could be grey: lit, unsaturated, and within `zoneDistance` of the sensor's
white point locus (read from `colourGains`), weighted by brightness. With
fewer than 8 such zones the whole frame is used, as before.

The rest is libipa's: the IMX681 and OV13858 tuning files use the Bayes
AWB on their locus (the chromaticities of the CCM records in the modules'
Windows tuning files), which keeps the estimate within 0.06 of the curve
and reads the colour temperature for the CCM from it. Before the port to
libipa this was the simple IPA's own locus projection with the same
offLocus (0.06) and smoothing (0.2).
